@@ -419,3 +419,30 @@ multica issue create --title "Step 1" --parent <issue-id> --assignee <agent> --s
 multica issue create --title "Step 2" --parent <issue-id> --assignee <agent> --stage 2 --status backlog
 multica issue create --title "Step 3" --parent <issue-id> --assignee <agent> --stage 3 --status backlog
 ```
+
+
+## Published workflow intake and issue cancellation
+
+The published-workflow candidate adds an authenticated
+POST /api/workspaces/{id}/workflow-intake adapter. It accepts source/event_id,
+template_key, title, description, optional payload/source_url and an
+idempotency key. It does not fetch source_url. Machine credentials are refused
+until their full provenance can be carried; task CLI identity restrictions
+remain unchanged. Callback, instance/debug controls and explicit version
+selection are rejected. An owner/admin assigning another member does not gain
+that member's private agent permissions: each routed step checks both humans.
+
+An active workflow owns its issue's status. Repeating the same normalized
+status is a no-op; other non-cancelling status changes return
+workflow_run_active. Moving the issue to cancelled or a custom closed-category
+status atomically updates the issue, cancels the run/steps/acceptances and writes
+events. Field validation, revision checks and text conflicts can reject the
+whole transaction without cancelling the run. Workflow status batches validate
+all targets before committing; a rejected target prevents partial cancellation.
+Historical terminal runs do not permanently lock an issue's status.
+
+TaskService stops only tasks linked through the cancelled workflow's steps.
+Stopping is attempted after commit and retried by reconciliation after failure
+or restart. A cancelled database row is cancellation intent, not proof that the
+daemon's process tree has exited. Direct run cancellation keeps its existing
+issue-status projection contract; it does not promise to set the issue status.
